# Copy to bridge/.env and fill in. This file is gitignored, because the
# bottom half of it is a password.

# ── where the relay is ────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co

# Sign in as yourself. Preferred: the anon key is publishable, row-level
# security still applies, and no service-role key ends up on this disk.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
SUPABASE_EMAIL=you@example.com
SUPABASE_PASSWORD=

# Or, instead of the three lines above, reuse the key the coding runner has.
# It bypasses row-level security, so keep it off any machine but this one.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=

# ── what your assistant is ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exactly one of these. A command gets the question as $ASSISTANT_PROMPT, as
# $ASSISTANT_TRANSCRIPT, and as JSON on stdin; whatever it prints is the
# reply, streamed to your phone as it is printed.
#
# ASSISTANT_CMD=python C:\ai\jarvis.py
# ASSISTANT_CMD=node bridge/example-assistant.mjs
#
# An endpoint is sent {messages, prompt, input, stream} and may answer with
# SSE, newline-delimited JSON, one JSON body, or plain text.
#
# ASSISTANT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat
# ASSISTANT_HEADERS={"authorization":"Bearer local-only"}

ASSISTANT_CMD=node bridge/example-assistant.mjs

# ── what the dashboard calls it ───────────────────────────────────────
# Both optional. The name defaults to this computer's name.
ASSISTANT_NAME=jarvis
ASSISTANT_MODEL=

# How many earlier messages to send with each question. Lower it if your
# assistant has a small context window.
# ASSISTANT_HISTORY=24

# ── your smart home hub ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional, and independent of the assistant above: set these and the same
# bridge also mirrors your house into the dashboard and obeys what you tap
# there. The hub is assumed to be Home Assistant on your Pi.
#
# Worth knowing before you fill this in: a relay here is reachable only
# while this PC is awake, and a PC is asleep exactly when you are out and
# want the hall light. The same relay runs *on the hub*, which is already on
# all the time — `npm run addon` for a Home Assistant OS install, or
# bridge/install-on-pi.sh for one with systemd. Running it in both places is
# fine, and is the better setup: either can obey a tap, and the hub's wins
# the job of mirroring devices.
#
# HA_URL is its address on your own network. HA_TOKEN is a long-lived access
# token: Home Assistant → your profile → Security → Long-lived access tokens.
# It is a password for your house — it stays in this file, which is
# gitignored, and never reaches the browser.
#
# HA_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123
# HA_TOKEN=

# What the dashboard calls it. Defaults to the hub's own location name.
# HA_NAME=home

# A real install has hundreds of diagnostic sensors (wifi strength, uptime,
# update available). By default only the readings a house screen is for are
# mirrored; `all` keeps every one of them.
# HA_SENSORS=all

# A regex of entity ids to leave out entirely.
# HA_EXCLUDE=^light\.test_

# How often to look at the hub, and how long to wait for it.
# HA_POLL_MS=10000
# HA_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# ── which relay this is ──────────────────────────────────
# All optional, and only interesting if you run a relay in more than one
# place. Each relay gets its own row on the House screen, and the one the
# dashboard believes is the one that can see the hub, on the hub, highest
# priority — in that order.
#
# Its name on that screen. Defaults to this computer's name.
# HOUSE_RELAY=desktop
#
# Whether this relay is on the hub itself. Guessed from HA_URL — you only
# reach a hub over loopback when you are on it — so normally leave it be.
# HOUSE_RELAY_HOST=pc
#
# Who wins the job of mirroring devices. Defaults to 10 on the hub and 0 on
# a PC, so the hub leads and the PC stands by and obeys taps.
# HOUSE_RELAY_PRIORITY=0
